      "problem": "Given that $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{n},$ what is $n?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ {({x}^{3})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {x}^{3\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $n=\\frac{3}{5}.$",
